<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sanatruq I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2nd century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sanatruq I was ruler of Hatra in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> in the mid-2nd century CE and the first to take the title 'king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>'. Under his dynasty the city, a Parthian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a>, built its great temples and walls and withstood Roman sieges. He is known from inscriptions and statues found at Hatra.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sanatruq-i.jpg" alt="Sanatruq I" width="320" height="254" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sanatruq_I,_2nd_century_AD._From_Hatra._Erbil_Civilization_Museum,_Iraqi_Kurdistan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sanatruq, also spelled Sanatruk, was the son of Naṣru, who governed Hatra from about 128 to 140. His name, meaning he who defeats enemies, is of Iranian origin and was typical of the Parthian royal family, a sign of the strong Parthian influence in the city. He reigned from about 140 to 180 and is known from more than 20 inscriptions found at Hatra, of which only one is dated, to the year 176/177.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He was one of the first rulers of Hatra to call himself malka, king, though he also bore the title mryʾ, administrator. His Parthian overlord Vologases IV granted him the title of king as the city grew in strategic importance. In the 2nd century Hatra flourished as a religious and commercial centre, well fortified enough to have already turned back three attacks by the Roman emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a>, and it remained a Parthian dependency until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> arose in 224.</p><p>Both of his titles are also recorded for his brother Wolgash, and it is unclear whether the two reigned together. The Square Building of Hatra was most likely built under Sanatruq I; it bears many similarities to the later Sasanian fire temples.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Sanatruq I was succeeded by Abdsamiya, whose relation to him is uncertain: there is no evidence that they belonged to the same dynasty, and Abdsamiya never used his father's name. The successor of Abdsamiya, however, was named Sanatruq II, which may suggest that the line of Sanatruq I continued. The statues commemorating Sanatruq I and his dynasty were not removed, which indicates that any change of dynasty was non-violent.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sanatruq I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its rulers at first bore the title 'lord' (mry'), and from the mid-2nd century CE, under <b>Sanatruq I</b>, they styled themselves 'king of the Arabs'.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hatra">Kingdom of Hatra</a></li></ul></section>
